messages.content:
This screen belongs to a clickable mockup of: A tiny bakery website

Show this screen: Details screen of the Cardamom Bun, a bakery item with price, ingredients and availability

Screens of this mockup, as a tree:
@home: the first screen of A tiny bakery website
  @menu: The menu screen, opened by "Order now"
  @cardamom-bun-details: Details screen of the Cardamom Bun, a bakery item with price, ingredients and availability (this screen)
  @sourdough-details: Details screen of the Sourdough loaf, a bakery item with price, ingredients and availability (not made yet)
  @cart: The cart screen (not made yet)
    @checkout: The checkout screen (not made yet)
      @order-confirmation: The order confirmation screen (not made yet)

The spec of this mockup: decisions every screen follows, one per line; a row with ? is not decided yet.
product.core-flow = @home → @menu → @cart → @checkout → @order-confirmation
ui.nav = Tabs: Home @home, Menu @menu, Cart @cart
ui.back ? how Back looks and where it sits
ui.lists ? how bakery items, prices, and availability appear in lists
ui.forms ? how fields, validation, and primary buttons are laid out
ui.colors ? which colors indicate primary actions, errors, and success
data.prices ? which currency is used and whether displayed prices include tax
data.fulfillment ? whether orders are for pickup or delivery and how timing is described

Tabs of ui.nav: last on every screen, in this markup, one link per tab with the label and @name ui.nav gives it, exactly; the app marks the current tab: <nav data-ui="tabs"><a data-action="@name">Label</a>…</nav>. A ui.nav line for a tab bar starts with Tabs: and names each tab by its label and @name; other navigation is written another way and gets no tab bar.
Back of ui.back: where ui.back says, with its label, in this markup: <a data-ui="back" data-action="back">Label</a>. The tab screens have no Back. A ui.back line starts with the label Back shows, then where it sits.

Design it for a phone screen, 390 px wide.

Design this as a clearly different alternative to the obvious layout: arrange this screen's content another way, not just the wording, and keep whatever the spec decides.

